Subject: [PATCH] quota: match Chrome's incognito quota, which Finch enables

Stock Chrome returns usage + 10 GiB for navigator.storage.estimate().quota in
incognito, the same constant it returns in regular mode. Our build returned a
value derived from physical memory, because kIncognitoStaticStorageQuota is
FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT in the tree and stock Chrome enables it through its
Finch seed, which we do not have.

Found by arithmetic that did not add up. The reference MacBook Pro has 36 GiB
installed. CalculateNominalDynamicSettings computes the incognito pool as
physical_memory * rand(0.15, 0.2), and CalculateReportedQuota returns the pool
rounded up when it falls below the 10 GiB cap — so 36 GiB predicts 6 to 8 GiB.
The Mac reports exactly 10737418240. Reproducing the prediction on our own build
with the same 36 GiB figure gave 7 GiB, three runs, stable; adding
--enable-features=IncognitoStaticStorageQuota gave 10737418240, matching the Mac
exactly.

This is the mirror image of the field-trial testing config problem fixed
earlier. Removing that config stopped us forcing features ON that Chrome has
off. It did not give us the features Finch turns ON that the compiled-in default
has off, and this is one. Three populations exist: stock Chrome runs its seed, a
fork runs the compiled-in defaults, and a fork that leaves the testing config
enabled runs a third state different from both.

There is no published list of features whose Finch state differs from their
default, so they have to be found by measuring stock Chrome against our build on
one machine and diffing. That is now a routine check rather than an accident.

Note this makes patch 0022 inert for the reported quota, which is the correct
outcome rather than a regression: with the static quota enabled the reported
value no longer derives from physical memory at all. 0022 still supplies the
profile's memory to the pool size, which continues to drive available_space, and
would still be needed if this feature were ever turned off.

Ledger: storage.incognito-quota.
diff --git a/storage/browser/quota/quota_features.cc b/storage/browser/quota/quota_features.cc
index 711c811..60a6b2e 100644
--- a/storage/browser/quota/quota_features.cc
+++ b/storage/browser/quota/quota_features.cc
@@ -39,7 +39,22 @@ BASE_FEATURE(kStaticStorageQuota, base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 
 // If enabled, Incognito mode will always report a maximal quota value to avoid
 // fingerprinting and incognito detection.
-BASE_FEATURE(kIncognitoStaticStorageQuota, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+// Enabled here, against the upstream default, because stock Chrome enables it
+// through its Finch seed and we have no seed.
+//
+// Measured: a MacBook Pro with 36 GiB installed reports exactly usage + 10 GiB
+// in incognito. With this feature off, the quota is derived from physical
+// memory — pool_size = physical_memory * rand(0.15, 0.2), reported as the pool
+// rounded up when it falls under the 10 GiB cap — which for 36 GiB predicts 6
+// to 8 GiB. Our build produced 7 GiB, three runs, stable. Enabling this
+// produced 10737418240, matching the Mac exactly.
+//
+// This is the mirror image of the field-trial testing config problem. Removing
+// that config stopped us forcing features ON that Chrome has off; it did not
+// give us the features Finch turns ON that the compiled-in default has off.
+// Chrome's effective configuration is its seed, and matching it means finding
+// these one at a time by measurement, because there is no published list.
+BASE_FEATURE(kIncognitoStaticStorageQuota, base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 
 }  // namespace features
 }  // namespace storage
